</div></section>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="external-storage"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>External drive: local and deliberate</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted"><strong>Example:</strong> an external drive connected to the computer for a scheduled backup, then disconnected and stored safely. This can suit a large local file collection or a home with limited internet availability.</p><ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-muted"><li><strong>Routine:</strong> you must connect the drive and allow the backup to complete.</li><li><strong>Capacity:</strong> available space and the way older versions are retained need checking.</li><li><strong>Availability:</strong> files may be quickly reachable when the drive is connected.</li><li><strong>Disconnected protection:</strong> separation can reduce exposure to some computer-side events, while forgetting to reconnect creates a gap.</li></ul><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A drive kept beside the computer can share the same theft, fire or spill. A local destination can also be lost, damaged or unavailable. It is not automatically safer simply because it is nearby.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="cloud-storage"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Cloud: accessible but account-dependent</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted"><strong>Example:</strong> selected folders copied or synchronised to a cloud account while the computer has internet access. This can suit someone who needs access away from home or does not want to manage a physical destination.</p><ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-muted"><li><strong>Routine:</strong> check that uploads finish and that the correct folders are selected.</li><li><strong>Internet:</strong> online-only files may not be available during an outage or without a download.</li><li><strong>Account access:</strong> protect the account and keep the recovery path current without placing credentials in backup notes.</li><li><strong>Versions:</strong> confirm what history or deleted-file retention is available.</li><li><strong>Sync boundary:</strong> a synchronised deletion may travel to other locations, so sync is not automatically an independent backup.</li></ul><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For example, <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/onedrive/save-disk-space-with-onedrive-files-on-demand-for-windows">OneDrive can show online-only files and synchronise deletions</a>. Ask whether a chosen cloud service retains a recoverable copy independently of changes on your computer. Version history and deleted-file retention have limits; neither should be assumed to last forever.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">On a Mac, <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/ways-to-back-up-or-protect-your-files-mh9052/mac">Apple distinguishes Time Machine, iCloud Drive and iCloud Photos</a>. Seeing selected files in iCloud does not establish a complete Mac backup.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="decision-check"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Make the choice operational</h2>
<div class="mb-8 overflow-x-auto" role="region" aria-label="Backup options comparison" tabindex="0"><table role="table" class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm md:text-base"><thead scope="col" class="border border-line bg-surface p-4 font-bold text-ink"><tr><th scope="col" class="border border-line bg-surface p-4 font-bold text-ink">Question</th><th data-label="External drive" scope="col" class="border border-line bg-surface p-4 font-bold text-ink">External drive</th><th data-label="Cloud" scope="col" class="border border-line bg-surface p-4 font-bold text-ink">Cloud</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label="Question" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Can the routine happen reliably?</td><td data-label="External drive" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Requires connection and a completed run.</td><td data-label="Cloud" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Requires account, internet and completed upload.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Question" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Can older versions be used?</td><td data-label="External drive" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Check the backup system's history settings.</td><td data-label="Cloud" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Check the provider's version and deletion retention.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Question" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Can files be reached offline?</td><td data-label="External drive" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Usually when the drive is connected.</td><td data-label="Cloud" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Only if a local copy is available.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Choose the option whose limitations you can manage. A cloud copy can separate files from a household incident, but account loss and provider problems still matter. A second independent destination can address some shared risks; it is a separate step beyond this one-destination starting plan. Then test one disposable file using <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/test-a-backup-file-restore/">the restore procedure</a>. A test is more useful than an assumption about the label “backup”.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="sample-check"><span aria-hidden="true">04</span>Check before you rely on it</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Create a new plain-text sample with a known sentence, place it in an included local folder, wait for a completed backup and restore a separate copy. Do not test by deleting an original or changing an important folder.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If the destination, version or restore path is unclear, stop and consult the current instructions for that system. Record what worked, when it worked and where the restored copy landed.</p>
